\title{The Geometry of Attention Sparsification: Concentration Floors,
Tail Ceilings, and the Concavity of the Selection Gap}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Top-$k$ attention replaces each row of an attention matrix by its $k$ largest entries,
trading accuracy for a cost reduction of $\ctx/k$. The width at which accuracy first clears
a fixed bar --- the \emph{knee} $k^{*}$ --- is empirical, but the laws constraining it are
not. We develop a self-contained theory of the knee for a single attention row, viewed as a
probability vector $p$ on $n$ keys, and of the \emph{selection gap} $G(k)=M(k)-k/n$
between the optimal width-$k$ selection and a uniformly random one. We prove: a
concentration floor $k \ge \tau^{2}\eff(p)$ with $\eff(p)=1/\sum_i p_i^{2}$, whose exponent
$2$ is necessary; a power-tail ceiling $M(k)\ge 1-\frac{c}{\alpha-1}k^{1-\alpha}$ for
$p_{(i)}\le c\,i^{-\alpha}$, $\alpha>1$, hence
$k^{*}\le\bigl(\tfrac{c}{(\alpha-1)(1-\tau)}\bigr)^{1/(\alpha-1)}$; the exact identity
$\mathbb{E}\sum_{i\in R}p_i = k/n$ for a uniformly random width-$k$ set $R$; concavity
$M(k+2)+M(k)\le 2M(k+1)$ by a one-step exchange, whence the gap is concave, unimodal and
nonnegative, with a falsifiable chord bound; and that the gap peaks exactly at
$k=\lvert\{i:p_i>1/n\}\rvert$ with height $\sum_i (p_i-1/n)^{+}=\TV(p,\unif)$, capping the
accuracy advantage of \emph{any} selection scheme by $L\cdot\TV$ under an $L$-Lipschitz
response. We further analyse the sweep protocol (upward-closed pass predicates, fail/pass
brackets, unique grid points, an attained resolution bound) and the concave depth law
$k^{*}(d)=Cd^{2/3}$, whose per-doubling factor $2^{2/3}\in(1.58,1.59)$ is sub-linear and
which forces any affine extrapolation to over-predict. At a measured cell (depth $32$,
context $512$) the knee is $256$ at two seeds, the concentration floor independently forces
$k>183$, the rows lie at total-variation distance at least $0.422$ from uniform, the law
$24.7\,d^{2/3}$ predicts $249$, the affine model $8d+32=288$ over-predicts by more than
$11\%$, the product law yields speedup exactly $1$, and the measured knee yields exactly
$2.0\times$.
\end{abstract}

