\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}
A causal transformer at context length $\ctx$ evaluates, per query position, a score against
every admissible key position and normalises the result into a probability vector. The
dominant cost is quadratic in the context. \emph{Top-$k$ attention} keeps only the $k$
largest entries of each row; the cost becomes $\ctx\cdot k$ score evaluations and the
speedup is exactly $\ctx/k$.

The engineering question is where to set $k$. Empirically one sweeps a grid of widths and
reports the smallest width whose downstream metric clears a bar: the \emph{knee} $k^{*}$.
The mathematical question, treated here, is what constrains the knee. Three kinds of
constraint are provable: geometric constraints on a single attention row (Sections
\ref{sec:setup}--\ref{sec:twosided}); constraints on the control experiment against random
selection (Sections \ref{sec:control}--\ref{sec:peak}); and constraints on the measurement
protocol itself (Section \ref{sec:protocol}).

